 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dd_acNdJVt3/",
 "title": "Launch a show, not a page",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator suggests treating an Instagram page like a TV show rather than just a collection of random posts. By using a repeatable format and building anticipation for future episodes, creators can keep their audience hooked just like TV series do.",
 "key_points": [
  "Don't just launch an Instagram page; launch a TV show that is ninety seconds long.",
  "People stay loyal to TV serials because of the same time, same characters, same format, and the expectation of what happens next.",
  "Instead of doing a daily 'new arrival' post like a saree shop owner, change it to a 'Ninety Second Wedding Fit' format every Sunday with one real customer.",
  "A repeatable format builds anticipation, making followers wait for the next episode and ask who is next week in the comments.",
  "You don't need a big camera or a large budget for this; you just need a repeatable format that makes people look forward to the next episode.",
  "A post asks for attention, but a show gets an appointment."
 ],
 "content": "Don't launch an Instagram page. Launch a TV show. Ninety seconds long. Why does your mother never miss a single day of her serial? Same time, same characters, same format. The expectation of what happens next. That's the power of a show. More than the content, that expectation is what keeps people hooked. Now look at a saree shop owner. Daily 'New arrival' post. Who's watching? Mom, aunt, the shopkeeper next door. The same owner makes one small change: 'Ninety Second Wedding Fit'. Every Sunday, one real customer. The timer's running. One saree, one blouse, one piece of jewellery, and the full bridal look has to be ready within ninety seconds. At the end, all the staff clap. Now followers wait for Sunday. In the comments: 'Who's next week?' That's not a page, man. Show. You don't need a big camera or a budget for this. You just need a repeatable format, something that makes people look forward to the next episode. Take an old TV format and make your shop's version of it. A post asks for attention. A show gets an appointment. Thank you.",
 "status": "done",
